WEBVTT

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:05.380
Treat forty five and thirty five as
alternative outcomes for one illustrative

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:05.990
request.

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:10.470
Each case starts from weights ten,
five and bias twenty,

00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:12.950
so both initial predictions are forty.

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:15.970
The loop stores returned
updates separately.

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:18.850
It does not assign them back
to the starting parameters.

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:23.940
Starting the second case from the previous
update would instead ask about successive

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:25.230
training steps.

00:00:25.640 --> 00:00:28.690
That would mix an outcome
change with a parameter change.

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:33.820
Holding the initial state fixed makes
the effect of the changed target easy to

00:00:33.820 --> 00:00:34.270
isolate.

00:00:36.482 --> 00:00:39.672
Forty minus thirty five is positive five.

00:00:40.062 --> 00:00:42.712
The model now predicts
five rentals too many.

00:00:43.602 --> 00:00:45.962
Half squared error is
still twelve point five,

00:00:45.962 --> 00:00:48.732
since the squared residual
magnitude is unchanged.

00:00:49.562 --> 00:00:54.392
Multiply the positive residual by the
two positive inputs to obtain gradients

00:00:54.722 --> 00:00:56.392
positive five and positive ten.

00:00:57.342 --> 00:00:59.612
The bias derivative is positive five.

00:01:00.102 --> 00:01:04.362
Each positive derivative says that
increasing that parameter slightly would

00:01:04.362 --> 00:01:05.712
raise the current loss.

00:01:06.402 --> 00:01:09.312
Gradient descent therefore
takes the opposite direction,

00:01:09.442 --> 00:01:12.612
while keeping its subtraction
rule and learning rate unchanged.

00:01:14.983 --> 00:01:18.753
Apply rate zero point zero
one to the first gradient.

00:01:19.243 --> 00:01:24.013
Ten minus zero point zero
five is nine point nine five.

00:01:24.623 --> 00:01:30.093
For the second weight, five minus zero
point one is four point nine zero.

00:01:30.983 --> 00:01:31.853
For the bias,

00:01:32.123 --> 00:01:37.133
twenty minus zero point zero
five is nineteen point nine five.

00:01:37.863 --> 00:01:40.773
All three changes are
downward in this case.

00:01:41.203 --> 00:01:45.133
They have the same magnitudes as the
upward changes for target forty five.

00:01:46.003 --> 00:01:50.763
The equality comes from changing only
the sign of the starting residual while

00:01:50.763 --> 00:01:54.533
keeping its magnitude and
both positive inputs fixed.

00:01:56.937 --> 00:02:00.367
Recompute using the new weights
and the same two inputs.

00:02:00.857 --> 00:02:02.947
The result is thirty
nine point seven zero.

00:02:03.657 --> 00:02:07.007
The first weighted contribution
falls by zero point zero five,

00:02:07.457 --> 00:02:12.107
the second by zero point two, and the
bias by another zero point zero five.

00:02:12.697 --> 00:02:15.667
Together they lower the
prediction by zero point three.

00:02:16.257 --> 00:02:19.007
The remaining overprediction
is four point seven zero,

00:02:19.417 --> 00:02:21.507
with loss eleven point zero four five.

00:02:22.417 --> 00:02:26.147
One update reduced the error;
it did not have to land on thirty five.

00:02:26.877 --> 00:02:29.567
The rate specifies a local parameter step,

00:02:29.577 --> 00:02:32.147
not a promise to solve
the example immediately.

00:02:34.498 --> 00:02:37.808
Put the original and
changed cases side by side.

00:02:38.278 --> 00:02:39.548
For target forty five,

00:02:39.958 --> 00:02:44.788
the prediction moves from forty to forty
point three, reducing a negative residual.

00:02:45.278 --> 00:02:49.968
For target thirty five, it moves from
forty to thirty nine point seven,

00:02:50.058 --> 00:02:51.588
reducing a positive residual.

00:02:52.318 --> 00:02:56.688
Both losses fall from twelve point
five to eleven point zero four five.

00:02:57.538 --> 00:03:02.648
Their gradients and changes have opposite
signs because their initial residuals do.

00:03:03.118 --> 00:03:06.388
This comparison is a
controlled numerical example.

00:03:06.838 --> 00:03:10.628
It does not measure how well a model
predicts a future bike dataset.

00:03:12.692 --> 00:03:17.582
The statement that both weights go down
depends on these inputs being positive.

00:03:18.112 --> 00:03:19.182
In a different example,

00:03:19.332 --> 00:03:23.552
a negative input would reverse its
matching weight derivative relative to the

00:03:23.552 --> 00:03:24.622
residual sign.

00:03:24.872 --> 00:03:27.412
The bias derivative still
equals the residual,

00:03:27.412 --> 00:03:30.122
because its forward derivative is one.

00:03:31.012 --> 00:03:35.062
Always trace the residual,
the matching input and the subtraction.

00:03:35.432 --> 00:03:39.142
That explanation survives
changed feature signs and scales.

00:03:39.732 --> 00:03:42.762
Run the linked example to
inspect both saved cases,

00:03:43.172 --> 00:03:46.952
then continue to the real hourly data
when you can account for their different

00:03:46.952 --> 00:03:47.582
directions.

